Article 1: The People's Court shall accept the following civil disputes involving rural land contracting in accordance with law:
(1) disputes over contract agreements;
(2) disputes over infringement of contractual management rights;
(3) disputes over infringement of land management rights;
(4) disputes over exchange or transfer of contractual management rights;
(5) disputes over circulation of land management rights;
(6) disputes over distribution of compensation fees for expropriation of contracted land;
(7) disputes over succession of contractual management rights;
(8) disputes over succession of land management rights.
Where a member of a rural collective economic organization brings a civil action on the ground that he or she has not actually obtained the contractual management right of land, the People's Court shall inform him or her to apply to the relevant administrative department for resolution.
Where a member of a rural collective economic organization brings a civil action regarding the amount of land compensation fees to be distributed, the People's Court shall not accept it.

Article 4: Where a farmer household has multiple members, litigation shall be conducted by its representative.
The representative of a farmer household shall be determined according to the following circumstances:
(1) the person recorded on the certificate of the right to land contractual management or other certificates;
(2) where a certificate of the right to land contractual management or other certificates has not been obtained through registration in accordance with law, the person who signed the contract agreement;
(3) where the person specified in items (1) and (2) is deceased, has lost capacity for civil conduct, or is otherwise unable to conduct litigation, the person elected by the household members.
II. Handling of Disputes over Household Contracting

Article 9: Where, before the contract-awarding party withdraws the contracted land in accordance with Article 27 of the Rural Land Contracting Law, the contracted party has already circulated the land management right to a third party by lease, shareholding, or other forms, and the circulation term has not yet expired, disputes arising from the collection of the circulation price shall be handled separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) where the contracted party has collected the circulation price in a lump sum, and the contract-awarding party requests the contracted party to return the circulation price for the remaining circulation term, the request shall be supported;
(2) where the circulation price is payable in installments, and the contract-awarding party requests the third party to pay the circulation price in accordance with the provisions of the circulation contract, the request shall be supported.

Article 11: In the circulation of land management rights, where a member of the collective economic organization claims a right of priority under conditions where the main contents such as the circulation price and the circulation term are the same, the claim shall be supported, except under the following circumstances:
(1) where the member has not raised the claim for priority within a reasonable period after written public announcement;
(2) where there is no written public announcement, and the member has not raised the claim for priority within two months from the date on which a person outside the collective economic organization begins to use the contracted land.

Article 19: Where the contract-awarding party signs two or more contract agreements for the same land, and the contracted parties all claim to have obtained the land management right, the matter shall be handled separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) the contracted party that has been registered in accordance with law obtains the land management right;
(2) where none has been registered in accordance with law, the contracted party under the contract that takes effect first obtains the land management right;
(3) where it cannot be determined in accordance with the preceding two items, the person who has lawfully possessed and used the contracted land in accordance with a contract agreement obtains the land management right, but the act and fact of one party forcibly taking prior possession of the contracted land after the dispute arises shall not be used as the basis for determining the land management right.
